#Branching Workflow

Step 1
git checkout -b <new branch name here> - This becomes the child of the main branch and has all its DNA,
that is, a copy of its version history (all commits).

Step 2
git checkout -b <new branch number 2 name here> - Same logic from step one applies here. Note, this ONLY has
the DNA from the new branch in step 1, NOT from the main branch. The new branch number 2 is the child of the
new branch (in step 1) and has no connection or affiliation with the main branch (the parent of its parent).

#Merging Work-Flow

Step 1
git diff <current working branch name desired to merge changes into here> <file name with the changes to merge here>

Step 2
git merge <file name with changes to merge here> - IMPORTANT - you can ONLY  merge to your current working branch,
hence the destination is not specified in the command but only the source. If your not sure of your working branch,
run git branch, git checkout <desired branch name>, and git branch to confirm again.

#Git Commands Use Specifications
Commands for Remote Interaction: push, pull, fetch, clone. Local and remote communication ONLY.
Commands for Local Branch Interaction: merge, checkout, rebase, and diff. local repository communication ONLY.
